    "answer": "Based on my thorough investigation, here are the comprehensive findings and constraints for virtualizing watchdog test waits.\n\n## Findings\n\n**Production code already has `TimeProvider` injected via DI.** The `ActivityWatchdog` class (in `src/VisualRelay.Core/Execution/ProcessRunners.Watchdog.cs`) already accepts an optional `TimeProvider?` parameter (line 78) and defaults to `TimeProvider.System` (line 85). It uses `_timeProvider.GetTimestamp()` instead of `time.monotonic` and `Task.Delay(TimeSpan, _timeProvider, ct)` instead of `time.sleep`. This means the \"Abstract TimeProvider with DI\" option is already partially implemented \u2014 a virtual `TimeProvider` can be injected into the existing constructor without touching production code.\n\n**Pure decision logic is already extracted.** Both `DecideOutcome` (line 162) and `TryDecideSocketWedge` (line 143) are static pure functions that take simulated elapsed/silence values. They are already extensively tested with synchronous, microsecond-speed unit tests \u2014 no real time needed. This pattern successfully eliminated real-time dependencies for decision-logic tests.\n\n**Remaining real-time tests fall into two categories.** Category A: `WaitAsync` loop tests that actually run the polling loop \u2014 these use `Task.Delay` pulse pumps (with real `CancellationToken`) and a real `Stopwatch` to measure how long the watchdog takes. Category B: Integration tests that spawn a real child process (`exec tail -f /dev/null`) via `ProcessCapture.RunAsync` and let the real watchdog kill it \u2014 these involve real OS processes that cannot be fully virtualized.\n\n**No virtual time provider exists in the codebase.** There is no `FakeTimeProvider`, `ManualTimeProvider`, or `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing` package referenced. A custom `ManualTimeProvider` would need to be written or the NuGet package `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing` (which provides `FakeTimeProvider`) would need to be added.\n\n**The `RealSleepGuard` enforces sleep-free tests.** A Roslyn-based analyzer (`tools/VisualRelay.Guards/RealSleepGuard.cs`) flags any shell `sleep N` in string literals, `(\"sleep\",\"30\")` argv forms, and uncancellable `Task.Delay`/`Thread.Sleep` calls \u2265 1000ms. The live gate (`AllTestProjectCsFiles_AreSleepFree`) is enabled and would fail if new real sleeps are introduced.\n\n**The test project targets `net10.0` with xUnit v3.** `TimeProvider` is a built-in class in `System` namespace (no external package needed). Several existing tests use `[AvaloniaFact]`/`[AvaloniaTheory]` from Avalonia.Headless.XUnit, but the watchdog tests use plain `[Fact]` and `[Theory]`.\n\n**Key files involved.** Production: `src/VisualRelay.Core/Execution/ProcessRunners.Watchdog.cs` (the `ActivityWatchdog` class). Tests: `tests/VisualRelay.Tests/ActivityWatchdogSocketWedgeTests.cs`, `tests/VisualRelay.Tests/ActivityWatchdogSocketWedgeTests.SustainedIdle.cs`, `tests/VisualRelay.Tests/SwivalSubagentRunnerWatchdogTests.ActivityWatchdog.cs` (containing `WaitAsync_BurstThenTotalSilence_FiresAtInactivityDeadline`, `WaitAsync_CpuPulseMasksDeadline_SocketWedgeStillFires`, etc.), plus `SwivalSubagentRunnerWatchdogTests.CpuPulse.cs`, `SwivalSubagentRunnerWatchdogTests.TierWindows.cs`, and `CliWatchdogTests.cs`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"findings\": \"Production `ActivityWatchdog` already injects `TimeProvider` via its constructor (defaults to `TimeProvider.System`); uses `GetTimestamp()` and `Task.Delay(..., _timeProvider, ct)` \u2014 no raw `time.monotonic`/`time.sleep`. Pure decision functions (`DecideOutcome`, `TryDecideSocketWedge`) are already extracted as static methods and tested with simulated time values (synchronous, no real clock). The remaining real-time tests are: (a) `WaitAsync` loop tests that use `Task.Delay`-based pulse pumps and real `Stopwatch` measurements, and (b) integration tests that spawn real child processes via `ProcessCapture.RunAsync` (these involve OS processes and cannot be fully virtualized). No virtual time provider exists yet. A `ManualTimeProvider` must be written or `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing` (providing `FakeTimeProvider`) added as a NuGet dependency. The `Task.Delay` calls in pump helpers are short (<50ms) and carry a real `CancellationToken`, so they already pass the `RealSleepGuard`. The `ActivityWatchdog`'s polling-loop `Task.Delay` (capped at 200ms) already uses the injected `_timeProvider`, so a virtual `TimeProvider.Advance()` would make it return instantly.\",\n  \"constraints\": [\n    \"The `RealSleepGuard` gate (`AllTestProjectCsFiles_AreSleepFree`) bans shell `sleep`, argv-form `(\\\"sleep\\\",\\\"30\\\")`, and uncancellable `Task.Delay`/`Thread.Sleep` \u2265 1000ms in tests; new code must not introduce real sleeps.\",\n    \"Production `ActivityWatchdog` constructor signature must remain backward-compatible \u2014 existing production callers pass no `TimeProvider` and rely on the `TimeProvider.System` default.\",\n    \"Tests that spawn real child processes (`ProcessCapture.RunAsync` with `exec tail -f /dev/null`) exercise real OS process lifecycle and kill signals; those integration tests cannot be fully virtualized \u2014 only the watchdog's time dimension can be made fast.\",\n    \"The `ActivityWatchdog` is `internal` and the test project uses `InternalsVisibleTo` \u2014 only the test project can access it; a `ManualTimeProvider` helper belongs in the test project.\",\n    \"Pulse pumps (`StartCpuPulsePump`, `StartBurstyWedgePump`, `StartIdleWedgePump`) run as background tasks; a virtual `TimeProvider.Advance()` must coordinate between the pump's `Task.Delay` and the watchdog's polling `Task.Delay` \u2014 both use the same `TimeProvider` instance.\",\n    \"`TimeProvider.GetTimestamp()` returns `long` ticks with a `TimestampFrequency`, not raw milliseconds; a virtual provider must return consistent ticks that convert correctly to milliseconds via `TicksToMs`.\",\n    \"The project targets `net10.0`; `System.TimeProvider` is available natively \u2014 no external package needed for the base class, but `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider` (if used) requires adding a NuGet package reference.\",\n    \"The `CliWatchdogTests` (`TimeoutWatchdog`) is a separate tool with its own process-based timeout; it does NOT use `ActivityWatchdog` or `TimeProvider` and is out of scope for this task.\"\n  ]\n}\n```",
